#let abstract-content = [
Exergames and automation for rehabilitation have shown to be effective in providing personalized therapy to patients with various conditions. However, therapists' sense of agency in relation to AI-supported computational systems for exergame-based rehabilitation remains largely under-explored. With the aim of exploring this gap, the study investigates therapists sense of agency through a participatory Research through Design (RtD) approach by employing two main methods: (1) a contextual inquiry conducted through a case study (Brain Stimulation) including interviews and observation, and (2) individual participatory speculative co-design sessions. Eight participants in total, with an average of over 11.6 years of relevant experience were involved in the study: five participated in the contextual inquiry and five in the co-design session. The results from the affinity diagramming indicate that under certain conditions, therapists' maintain a sense of agency in AI supported computational systems for exergame-based, provided that their responsibility and care over their patient is not threatened. Although, these conditions change across the different phases of rehabilitation. Collectively, this study contributes to the idea of co-performance with AI systems, which is more narrow and accurate than the BIG more-than-human literature (which means so many different things: human-nature interactions, etc.)
]

